<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<TcPlcObject Version="1.1.0.1" ProductVersion="3.1.4024.12">
  <DUT Name="HMI_SP" Id="{dc1fe36a-0f14-4cbf-954b-42b3e62d5aea}">
    <Declaration><![CDATA[(* Setpoints managed by the HMI
	rules 
		setpoints can only be basic types (bool / byte / word / int / real / string)
			!don't use enums (you probably can but you hav to define it as a basic type and you then only see a number in the HMI)
		setpoints are only READ in the plc. Don't write to setpoints!
			The HMI may overwrite at any moment the setpoints (if the operator activates a recipe)
			The HMI will not detect a change in setpoints made in the plc >> such a change which isn't allowed, won't be auto detected and saved in the recipe
			The Bknlibrary functionality will issue a save persistent command at detection of a change (it will go banana's if setpoints keep constantly changing)
		setpoints should be used as setpoints... 
		
		General naming convention (idea is that during coding you can quickly find the correct setpoint)
			TB_UseExtraPickOffset	//togglebutton use extra pick offset
			DST_ExtraPickOffset		//extra pick offset distance
			POS_SrvPicker_Pick 		//pick position
			SPD_SrvPicker_Travel 	//travel speed
			TMR_PickTime			//pick delay
			
			note: PB_DoSomething >> BAD!!! A pushbutton is only momentarily high so the setpoint will always be false. If you manage to get it high in a recipe
				then at every HMI-recipe activation the button will be set...  Declare it in HMI_STS instead!  
		
		not listed >> don't use different schemes for the same thing and you are free to use your imagination
		note: if there are many setpoints in the same struct then try to group settings that belong to each other
			using above example it will become:
				Picker.TB_UseExtraPickOffset
				Picker.DST_ExtraPickOffset
				Picker.Pos_SrvPicker_Pick
				Picker.SPD_SrvPicker_Travel
				Picker.TMR_PickTime
			now you don't have to explain the HMI programmer anymore which setpoints belong to each other.	
*)
TYPE HMI_SP :
STRUCT
	General: HMI_SP_General;								(*General*)

	{info 'TODO TEMPLATE REMOVE: Example scam'}
	//Example for how to use, remove me
	Example:HMI_SP_TemplateMachinePart;
END_STRUCT
END_TYPE
]]></Declaration>
  </DUT>
</TcPlcObject>